-- title:
--   Igusa's lower bound for the mod-ℓ q-expansion field
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $M$ with $M \neq 0$ in the sense of `NeZero`, a subgroup $H$ of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$, and a prime $\ell$ not dividing $M$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $\ell$, in the sense that $\ell$ is a non-unit of $A$, and write $k =$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField A`. For a subgroup $\Gamma \le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC k Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101) denotes the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $k((q))$ generated over $k$ by all ratios $\bar p_f/\bar p_g$, where $f,g$ are modular forms of one and the same weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ whose $q$-expansions are given by integral power series $p_f,p_g$ and where the coefficientwise reduction $\bar p_g$ into $k[[q]]$ is non-zero; here $\Gamma$ is $\Gamma_H(M)$, the image in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of the set of $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M)$ whose lower-right entry reduces into $H$. Let $x$ be an element of this field whose underlying Laurent series is $q^{-1}$ times the reduction to $k$ of the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$, i.e. the reduction $\bar\jmath$ of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant. The conclusion is that the index in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of $\Gamma_{H'}(M)$, where $H'$ is the join of $H$ with the subgroup generated by $-1$, is at most the $k(x)$-dimension of [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC k (CohCarrier.GammaH M H)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), the adjunction $k(x)$ being formed inside that field.
--
--   This is the degree form of Igusa's theorem on good reduction of $X_H(M)$ at primes $\ell \nmid M$: the mod-$\ell$ $q$-expansion field is at least as large over $k(\bar\jmath)$ as the generic degree $[\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}) : \{\pm 1\}\Gamma_H(M)]$ of the covering $X_H(M) \to X(1)$. Combined with the opposite inequality it identifies the function field of the special fibre, and it is used in the analysis of places and specialisations of the modular curve, in particular in the cuspidality and prolongation results for $J$-places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_index_gammaH_le_finrank_adjoin_jqModC_qExpFunctionFieldC_residueField.lean

